---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GalleryPage from "../components/GalleryPage.jsx";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Gallery | Algorithmic Art Gallery by Jonathan R Reed"
  description="Browse saved generative artworks from the Algorithmic Art Gallery by Jonathan R Reed, featuring space-filling curves and mathematical patterns."
>
  <div class="gallery-container">
    <h1 class="gallery-header">Gallery</h1>
    <p class="mono-text">
      By Jonathan R Reed. Updated <time datetime="2026-04-21">April 21, 2026</time>.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      Saved pieces collect the strongest algorithmic art runs from the generator.
      Use the gallery to revisit deterministic outputs, compare visual systems,
      and inspect how seed, symmetry, curve and color choices change each result.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      Each piece is treated as a reproducible study. The gallery gives finished
      outputs a place away from the live controls so visual decisions can be
      reviewed by composition, color balance, density, and pattern behavior.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      Return to the generator when a saved work suggests a new direction, then
      vary one setting at a time to understand how the system reacts.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      The strongest pieces usually come from a clear constraint: limited
      symmetry, a deliberate curve choice, or a palette that exposes the
      structure of the traversal. Use the collection as a reference wall for
      those experiments.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      Saved works also make the system easier to evaluate over time. Compare
      dense studies against quieter ones, watch how color ordering changes the
      perceived motion, and keep notes on the settings worth returning to.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      The page is intentionally a review space rather than another control panel.
      It helps separate finished compositions from active experimentation, making
      it easier to decide which outputs deserve export, reuse, or another pass
      through the generator with tighter constraints.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-intro">
      When a saved piece works, treat its settings as a recipe. Recreate it,
      change one variable, and compare the result against the original before
      moving on to a different visual direction.
    </p>
    <p class="gallery-links">
      <a href="/">Open generator</a>
      <a href="/about/">Read process notes</a>
    </p>
    <GalleryPage client:load />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
